response:
To further optimize efficiency for handling very large texts, you can consider using memory-mapped files instead of reading the entire text into memory at once. This allows you to process the text in chunks without needing to load it all into RAM. Here's how to modify the previous code to use memory-mapped files:

```matlab
function uniqueWords = findUniqueWords(fileName1, fileName2)
    %Read text files as memory-mapped files
    mmap1 = mmopen(fileName1, 'r', 'clobber');
    mmap2 = mmopen(fileName2, 'r', 'clobber');

    %Convert text to lowercase and split into words
    words1 = lower(mmtextscan(mmap1, '%s', []));
    words2 = lower(mmtextscan(mmap2, '%s', []));

    %Close memory-mapped files
    mmclose(mmap1);
    mmclose(mmap2);

    %Find unique words
    uniqueWords = setdiff(words1(:), words2(:));
end
```

In this modified function, `mmopen()` is used to open the text files as memory-mapped files, which allows us to read them in chunks without loading the whole contents into memory. The `mmtextscan()` function is then used to convert the text into cell arrays of words, with the `:` argument added to return the entire array instead of just the first element. After processing both files, we close the memory-mapped files using `mmclose()`. Note that you may need to adjust the size of the chunk being read using the `'chunksize'` option when opening the memory-mapped files if your text files are too large to fit into memory even after splitting into words.

