To handle this exception, you can add a try-catch block around the code that reads the input, as follows:

```java
import java.util.InputMismatchException;
import java.util.Scanner;

public class RetrieveInputExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);

        int number = 0;

        try {
            System.out.print("Enter an integer: ");
            number = scanner.nextInt();
        } catch (InputMismatchException e) {
            System.err.println("Invalid input! Please enter an integer.");
            scanner.nextLine(); // consume the non-integer token
            return;
        }

        System.out.println("The entered number is: " + number);

        scanner.close();
    }
}
```

In this updated example, we first import the `InputMismatchException` class from the `java.util` package. Then, inside the `try` block, we read the integer input using `Scanner.nextInt()`. If an `InputMismatchException` occurs during this operation, we catch it and print an error message. We also consume any remaining input on the current line using `Scanner.nextLine()` to avoid reading invalid input again. Finally, we exit the function using the `return` statement.

By handling this exception gracefully, your application will provide a better user experience and prevent unexpected behavior due to invalid input.

